WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.202
Microsoft Fabric gives us an environment where storage, notebooks, Spark, and analytical tables can work together.

00:00:08.202 --> 00:00:12.122
A common workflow begins with files in a lakehouse.

00:00:12.122 --> 00:00:15.262
A notebook reads those files with Spark.

00:00:15.262 --> 00:00:21.597
We clean and transform the data, perhaps using both data frames and Spark S Q L.

00:00:21.597 --> 00:00:25.442
Then we save a curated result as a reusable table.

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:29.878
Notice that the basic data-preparation process is still familiar.

00:00:29.878 --> 00:00:37.473
We inspect the source, define the grain, clean values, transform the structure, validate the result,

00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:45.161
and publish something useful. Fabric changes the environment, but it does not remove those responsibilities.
